      ":question/id": "431b9643-306d-5fab-92cf-5f6d4f6456c7",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y′=\\frac{1 - x}{y}.$ Work on intervals where $y\\ne 0$ and the radicand is positive.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y′ & =\\frac{1 - x}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =1 - x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int 1 - x\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\frac{y^{2}}{2} & =2 x - \\frac{\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}}{2} + 2+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2}=2 x - \\frac{\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}}{2} + 2+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ Solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^2 & =4 x - \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 4+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm\\sqrt{4 x - \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 4+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm\\sqrt{4 x - \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 4+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=2C$ is an arbitrary real constant.",
