      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y′=2 y^{2} + 2.$ Work on intervals avoiding tangent poles.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y′ & =2 y^{2} + 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y^{2} + 1}\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int 2\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\arctan{\\left(y \\right)} & =2 x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\arctan{\\left(y \\right)}=2 x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ Solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\ny=\\tan{\\left(C + 2 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant.",
